{
  "id": "c17",
  "title": "Linked Lists",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "c17-l1",
      "title": "Nodes",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "An array sits in one block of memory. A linked list is different: every element is a separate node from malloc, and each node points to the next one.",
        "struct node { int value; struct node *next; }; A struct may contain a pointer to its own kind. The last node's next is NULL: that's where the list ends.",
        "The program only keeps a pointer to the first node, the head. Everything else is reached by following next."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "#include <stdlib.h>",
          "",
          "struct node {",
          "    int value;",
          "    struct node *next;",
          "};",
          "",
          "struct node *make(int v) {",
          "    struct node *n = malloc(sizeof(struct node));",
          "    n->value = v;",
          "    n->next = NULL;",
          "    return n;",
          "}",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    struct node *head = make(1);",
          "    head->next = make(2);",
          "    head->next->next = make(3);",
          "    printf(\"%d %d %d\\n\", head->value, head->next->value, head->next->next->value);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "1 2 3"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
