    {
      "id": "c17-l2",
      "title": "Walking a List",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "To visit every node, start at the head and follow next until you hit NULL: for (p = head; p != NULL; p = p->next). This loop is everywhere in C.",
        "The easiest way to add a node is at the front: the new node points to the old head, and becomes the new head.",
        "That's why a list built that way comes out backwards: the last one added is first."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "#include <stdlib.h>",
          "",
          "struct node {",
          "    int value;",
          "    struct node *next;",
          "};",
          "",
          "struct node *push(struct node *head, int v) {",
          "    struct node *n = malloc(sizeof(struct node));",
          "    n->value = v;",
          "    n->next = head;",
          "    return n;",
          "}",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    struct node *head = NULL;",
          "    struct node *p;",
          "    int sum = 0;",
          "    head = push(head, 10);",
          "    head = push(head, 20);",
          "    head = push(head, 30);",
          "    for (p = head; p != NULL; p = p->next) {",
          "        printf(\"%d \", p->value);",
          "        sum += p->value;",
          "    }",
          "    printf(\"\\nsum %d\\n\", sum);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "30 20 10 ",
          "sum 60"
        ]
      },
